            <ArticleTitle>Thoracoscopic splanchnicectomy for pain relief in unresectable pancreatic cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>332-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="HYPOTHESIS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Unilateral truncal thoracoscopic splanchnicectomy (TS) provides safe and effective treatment for pain relief in patients with unresectable pancreatic cancer.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Before-and-after trial of 24 patients undergoing 25 TS procedures.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Surgical unit at a university teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A consecutive sample of 24 patients with severe pain due to unresectable (primary or recurrent) pancreatic cancer refractory to drug therapy and with a life expectancy of less than 6 months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">The key point of the reported operation is intrathoracic carbon dioxide insufflation, which allows a more distal division of the greater splanchnic nerve and a 2-port technique.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pain and the effect of this symptom on quality of life were assessed before and after TS using a 10-point visual analog pain scale (VAS) and the Nottingham Health Profile questionnaire, respectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Four TS procedures were technical failures because of pleural adhesions. One patient required a contralateral procedure 12 weeks after TS. Mean (+/- SD) preoperative VAS basal score was 7.4 +/- 1.7. Twenty-four hours after TS, it was reduced to 0.6 +/- 1.0. Significant reduction of VAS scores persisted over the first 3 months after TS (P&lt;.001). Recurrence of pain of low intensity (mean VAS basal score, 4.2) was observed in 8 patients. Significant improvement (P&lt;.001) in each area covered by the Nottingham Health Profile questionnaire was reported at 1 month after TS.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Thoracoscopic splanchnicectomy offered substantial short-term relief of pain in patients with unresectable pancreatic cancer, and significantly ameliorated the quality of their residual life.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
